        },
        "procedure": "Action; range 150 feet; V/S/F. KEEP an area appearing as another terrain. NEW choose a perceived supported terrain center within 150 feet and line of effect; the stationary horizontal footprint has radius up to seventy-five feet, so its maximum horizontal span is the printed 150 feet. Its visual depiction occupies at most thirty feet above or below the chosen center, clipped by real barriers and the terrain actually perceived when composed. Replace Instantaneous with concentration up to one hour. At casting specify one static visual terrain appearance based on actual known/perceived shapes: stone can appear grassy, safe ground can look broken, or visible shallow water can look like a dry bed. It does not reveal or accurately reconstruct concealed geography. NEW only background terrain appearance changes: creatures, independently carried objects, buildings, vehicles, doors, mechanisms and actual light sources remain visibly themselves; it cannot paint a concealing opaque surface over them. It may disguise a perceived natural pit or apparent footing, but the illusion provides no real floor. It does not remove actual visual obstruction: a real boulder painted as grass still blocks sight/line of effect and physically obstructs passage. A false boulder on empty ground adds no mechanical blocked sight, cover or collision. No sound, smell, temperature, touch, harvest, difficult-terrain modifier, navigation success or forced movement is created. Observers can make the shared examination or witness conclusive contradiction; an informed observer can distinguish the false appearance without seeing through real obstacles. The image is fixed to its initial world area and never follows a vehicle or relocates with uprooted terrain. Newly exposed ground or arriving objects are not newly mapped or given an intelligently adapted image; actual departures/changed objects can supply evidence of the false overlay. There is no independent image control after casting. Overlapping such depictions retain separate records; NEW at a shared visual surface only the latest successful depiction is presented, with an older still-active one becoming apparent if the later one ends. Knowing one does not automatically identify an independently different illusion. Suppression removes only the affected source appearance while its clock runs; concentration loss, expiry or removal ends it.",
        "selectedProcedureSource": {
          "candidate": {
